xy.py: "with open" - I used this too, nice use.  Nice job organizing your code with a function, clear comments, and regex!!! I wanted to include, but didn't have time to workout bugs.  You rock. Oh and yea with set to solve the problem of deduping cleanly - my fav.
wf.py: Your approach to opening using "with open", I like.  Your logic works and the step-by-step comments make it easy to follow. one improvement would be avoiding variable names like list and set - it is sometimes confusing on a casual glance.
md.py: This is a clean solution with (what I think is the ) correct use of set, and interesting use of lowercase. one small improvement is printing a short label with the result so users know what the number means.
gs.py: Might be good to add what the number is about on the output! Nice short/tight program with solid exception handling and lowercase-ing, i had issues using strip(string.punctuation) - seemed to only remove punctuation at the ends of words, so words with an ' inside (like can't), don't get the ' removed. 
n/a tf.py: Strong structure with main and then another function, and regex - I wish I had used this. Code worked.  
gx.py: You used good error handling and clear processing steps.  It WAS interesting...but printing every word/count wasn't a requirement. And a case statement?  Wow - not sure. 
mj.py: You have a clear approach, good comments, but there are a few fixes needed: the argument-check block is duplicated, and using with open(pathname) as file: would simplify file closing and make the code cleaner.